{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000117","statement":"Acute intermittent porphyria (AIP) is one of the liver (hepatic) porphyrias, caused by low levels of porphobilinogen deaminase (PBGD), an enzyme also called hydroxymethylbilane synthase.","source_span":"Acute intermittent porphyria (AIP) is one of the liver (hepatic) porphyrias. AIP is caused by low levels of porphobilinogen deaminase (PBGD), an enzyme also often called hydroxymethylbilane synthase."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000117","statement":"Activating factors such as hormones, drugs, and dietary changes may trigger AIP symptoms.","source_span":"activating factors such as hormones, drugs, and dietary changes may trigger symptoms"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000117","statement":"Symptomatic AIP individuals typically present with abdominal pain with nausea.","source_span":"symptomatic individuals typically present with abdominal pain with nausea"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000117","statement":"AIP symptoms usually appear after puberty, and activating factors include alcohol, drugs (e.g., barbiturates, steroids, sulfa-containing antibiotics), chemicals, smoking, reduced caloric intake, stress, and travel.","source_span":"Those individuals that present with symptoms usually do so after puberty, probably because of hormonal influences, although other activating factors include: alcohol, drugs (e.g., barbiturates, steroids, sulfa-containing antibiotics), chemicals, smoking, reduced caloric intake, stress, and travel."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000117","statement":"AIP symptoms usually last several days, but attacks that are not treated promptly may last weeks or months.","source_span":"Symptoms usually last several days, but attacks for which treatment is not received promptly may last weeks or months."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000117","statement":"Individuals with AIP have an increased risk of developing hepatocellular carcinoma, and some develop kidney failure.","source_span":"Individuals with AIP have an increased risk of developing hepatocellular carcinoma; some develop kidney failure."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000117","statement":"Abdominal pain occurs in about 90% of AIP patients according to HPO data.","source_span":"Abdominal pain 90%"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000117","statement":"AIP is caused by deficiency of PBGD due to a mutation in the HMBS gene, which is the only gene known to be associated with AIP.","source_span":"The deficiency of PBGD is caused by a mutation in the HMBS gene. The HMBS gene is the only gene known to be associated with AIP."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000117","statement":"AIP is inherited in an autosomal dominant fashion, meaning only one of the two HMBS genes needs a disease-causing mutation to decrease enzyme activity and cause symptoms.","source_span":"AIP is inherited in an autosomal dominant fashion, which means only one of the two HMBS genes needs to have a disease-causing mutation to decrease enzyme activity and cause symptoms."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000117","statement":"Diagnosis of an acute porphyria such as AIP is established by finding increased levels of delta-aminolevulinic acid (ALA) and porphobilinogen (PBG) in urine.","source_span":"The finding of increased levels of delta-aminolevulinic acid (ALA) and porphobilinogen (PBG) in urine establishes that one of the acute porphyrias is present."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000117","statement":"Molecular genetic testing of the HMBS gene detects more than 98% of affected individuals and is available in clinical laboratories.","source_span":"Molecular genetic testing of the HMBS gene detects more than 98% of affected individuals and is available in clinical laboratories."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000117","statement":"Panhematin, an intravenous medication used to correct heme deficiency, may be prescribed to treat AIP.","source_span":"Panhematin, an intravenous medication used to correct heme deficiency, may also be prescribed."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000117","fact_refs":[0,7],"question":"What enzyme is deficient in acute intermittent porphyria, and which gene mutation causes this deficiency?","answer":"AIP is caused by low levels of porphobilinogen deaminase (PBGD), also called hydroxymethylbilane synthase. This deficiency results from a mutation in the HMBS gene, the only gene known to be associated with AIP."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000117","fact_refs":[1,3],"question":"What factors can trigger symptoms in a person with acute intermittent porphyria?","answer":"Symptoms are typically triggered by activating factors such as hormones, drugs (e.g., barbiturates, steroids, sulfa-containing antibiotics), chemicals, alcohol, smoking, reduced caloric intake, stress, and travel, often appearing after puberty."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000117","fact_refs":[8],"question":"How is acute intermittent porphyria inherited?","answer":"AIP is inherited in an autosomal dominant fashion, meaning only one of the two HMBS genes needs a disease-causing mutation to decrease enzyme activity and cause symptoms."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000117","fact_refs":[4],"question":"How long do symptoms of an AIP attack typically last, and what happens if treatment is delayed?","answer":"Symptoms usually last several days, but if treatment is not received promptly, attacks may last weeks or months."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000117","fact_refs":[9,10],"question":"What lab findings are used to diagnose acute intermittent porphyria?","answer":"Increased levels of delta-aminolevulinic acid (ALA) and porphobilinogen (PBG) in urine establish that an acute porphyria is present, and molecular genetic testing of the HMBS gene detects more than 98% of affected individuals."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000117","fact_refs":[11],"question":"What intravenous medication is used to treat acute intermittent porphyria by correcting heme deficiency?","answer":"Panhematin, an intravenous medication that corrects heme deficiency, may be prescribed to treat AIP."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000147","statement":"Cancer of the appendix is very rare and is typically found incidentally during appendectomies, in about 1% of cases.","source_span":"Cancer of the appendix is very rare and is typically found incidentally during appendectomies, in about 1% of the cases."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000147","statement":"According to a National Cancer Institute report using the SEER database, appendix cancers account for about 0.4% of gastrointestinal tumors.","source_span":"According to a report published by the National Cancer Institute, using the Surveillance, Epidemiology, and End Results (SEER) database, appendix cancer account for about 0.4% of gastrointestinal tumors."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000147","statement":"The carcinoid type is the most common subtype of appendix cancer, accounting for 66% of the total.","source_span":"The most common is the carcinoid type (66% of the total)"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000147","statement":"Cyst-adenocarcinoma accounts for 20% and adenocarcinoma accounts for 10% of appendix cancer subtypes.","source_span":"with cyst-adenocarcinoma accounting for 20% and adenocarcinoma accounting for 10%"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000147","statement":"Signet cell carcinoma of the appendix is the rarer subtype, involving only 4% of all appendix cancer subtypes.","source_span":"signet cell carcinoma of appendix (which is the rarer involving only 4% of all the subtypes of appendix cancer)"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000147","statement":"Rare forms of appendix cancer include adenocarcinoid, signet ring, non-Hodgkins lymphoma, ganglioneuroma, and pheochromocytoma.","source_span":"rare forms of cancers which include adenocarcinoid, signet ring, non-Hodgkins lymphoma, ganglioneuroma, and pheochromocytoma"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000147","statement":"The most common clinical symptom of adenocarcinoma of the appendix is acute appendicitis.","source_span":"The most common clinical symptom is acute appendicitis."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000147","statement":"Other symptoms of adenocarcinoma of the appendix include a palpable abdominal mass, ascites, peritonitis due to a perforated appendix, bloating, vague abdominal pain, and tenderness.","source_span":"Other symptoms include a palpable abdominal mass, ascites (fluid buildup), peritonitis (inflammation of the membrane lining the abdominal cavity) due to a perforated appendix, and non-specific gastrointestinal or genitourinary symptoms such as bloating, vague abdominal pain, and tenderness."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000147","statement":"Mucinous adenocarcinomas of the appendix may be found incidentally as a right sided cystic mass on an imaging study.","source_span":"Mucinous adenocarcinomas may also be found incidentally as a right sided cystic mass on an imaging study."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000147","fact_refs":[0],"question":"How commonly is appendix cancer found, and how is it typically discovered?","answer":"Cancer of the appendix is very rare and is typically found incidentally during appendectomies, occurring in about 1% of cases."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000147","fact_refs":[1],"question":"What percentage of gastrointestinal tumors do appendix cancers account for, according to the SEER database?","answer":"According to a National Cancer Institute report using the SEER database, appendix cancers account for about 0.4% of gastrointestinal tumors."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000147","fact_refs":[2,3],"question":"What are the relative frequencies of the main subtypes of appendix cancer?","answer":"The carcinoid type is most common at 66% of cases, followed by cyst-adenocarcinoma at 20% and adenocarcinoma at 10%."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000147","fact_refs":[4],"question":"How rare is signet cell carcinoma among appendix cancer subtypes?","answer":"Signet cell carcinoma of the appendix is the rarer subtype, involving only 4% of all appendix cancer subtypes."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000147","fact_refs":[6,7],"question":"What are the common symptoms of adenocarcinoma of the appendix?","answer":"The most common clinical symptom is acute appendicitis. Other symptoms include a palpable abdominal mass, ascites, peritonitis from a perforated appendix, bloating, vague abdominal pain, and tenderness."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000147","fact_refs":[8],"question":"How might mucinous adenocarcinoma of the appendix appear on imaging?","answer":"Mucinous adenocarcinomas may be found incidentally as a right-sided cystic mass on an imaging study."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000148","statement":"Adenoid cystic carcinoma (ACC) is a rare form of adenocarcinoma, which is cancer that begins in glandular tissues.","source_span":"Adenoid cystic carcinoma (ACC) is a rare form of adenocarcinoma, which is cancer that begins in gladular tissues."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000148","statement":"ACC most commonly arises in the head and neck, in the major and minor salivary glands including the palate, nasopharynx, lining of the mouth, larynx, or trachea.","source_span":"ACC most commonly arises in the head and neck, in various parts of the major and minor salivary glands including the palate, nasopharynx, lining of the mouth, voice box (larynx) or windpipe (trachea)."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000148","statement":"ACC can also occur in the breast, uterus, or other locations in the body.","source_span":"It can also occur in the breast, uterus, or other locations in the body."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000148","statement":"Early symptoms of ACC may include lumps under the lining of the mouth or facial skin, numbness in the mouth or face, difficulty swallowing, hoarseness, pain, or paralysis of a facial nerve.","source_span":"Early symptoms depend on the tumor's location and may include lumps under the lining of the mouth or facial skin; numbness in the mouth or face; difficulty swallowing; hoarseness; pain; or paralysis of a facial nerve."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000148","statement":"ACC spreads along nerves or through the bloodstream, and only spreads to the lymph nodes in about 5-10% of cases.","source_span":"ACC spreads along nerves or through the bloodstream, and only spreads to the lymph nodes in about 5-10% of cases."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000148","statement":"The cause of ACC is currently unknown, and no strong genetic or environmental risk factors specific to ACC have been identified.","source_span":"The cause of ACC is currently unknown. ... no strong genetic or environmental risk factors specific to ACC have been identified."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000148","statement":"Treatment of ACC depends on many factors and may include surgery, radiation, and/or chemotherapy.","source_span":"Treatment depends on many factors and may include surgery, radiation, and/or chemotherapy."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000148","statement":"Only one case of apparent familial ACC has been reported worldwide, involving a father and daughter both affected with ACC of the sublingual salivary gland.","source_span":"only one case of apparent familial ACC has been reported worldwide. In this case, a father and daughter were both affected with ACC of the sublingual salivary gland."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000148","statement":"There has been one reported case of ACC of the salivary gland occurring in a person with basal cell nevus syndrome, a hereditary tumor-predisposition syndrome.","source_span":"There has also been one report of a case of ACC of the salivary gland occurring in a person with basal cell nevus syndrome, a hereditary syndrome known to predispose affected people to a very wide range of tumors."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000148","fact_refs":[0,1],"question":"What is adenoid cystic carcinoma and where does it most commonly arise?","answer":"Adenoid cystic carcinoma (ACC) is a rare form of adenocarcinoma beginning in glandular tissues, most commonly arising in the head and neck in the major and minor salivary glands, including the palate, nasopharynx, mouth lining, larynx, or trachea."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000148","fact_refs":[2],"question":"Besides the head and neck, where else can adenoid cystic carcinoma occur?","answer":"ACC can also occur in the breast, uterus, or other locations in the body."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000148","fact_refs":[3],"question":"What are some early symptoms of adenoid cystic carcinoma?","answer":"Early symptoms, depending on tumor location, may include lumps under the mouth lining or facial skin, numbness in the mouth or face, difficulty swallowing, hoarseness, pain, or facial nerve paralysis."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000148","fact_refs":[4],"question":"How does adenoid cystic carcinoma typically spread, and how often does it reach the lymph nodes?","answer":"ACC spreads along nerves or through the bloodstream, and only spreads to the lymph nodes in about 5-10% of cases."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000148","fact_refs":[5,6],"question":"Is the cause of adenoid cystic carcinoma known, and what treatments are available?","answer":"The cause of ACC is currently unknown, with no strong genetic or environmental risk factors identified. Treatment may include surgery, radiation, and/or chemotherapy depending on many factors."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000148","fact_refs":[7],"question":"Has a familial case of adenoid cystic carcinoma ever been reported?","answer":"Yes, only one case of apparent familial ACC has been reported worldwide, involving a father and daughter both affected with ACC of the sublingual salivary gland."}
